>
>> In any case, if I cannot be accused because I happen to remember what
>> ELBIB YLOH, HACKEM MUCHE, etc. stand for, why can I be accused if I
>> wrote it down?
>
>Once it's IDed, the game "writes it down" for you by changing the name
>in the interface.  Writing it down out of game as well serves no purpose
>except in preparation for amnesia.  

An additional benefit of the game interface as it is now is that it is
unlikely that you *will* recall the appearance of a particular wand,
since you will never view it as that appearance once it's id'd. Thus,
when the character "forgets" and the game interface returns to
describing the items by appearance, it's unlikely that you will recall
(without the use of notes) the identity of items by their appearance.

(An exception may be items which you've made special note of, because
their appearance is important for some reason: silver rings, for
example.)

However, inferring the identity of forgotten items by what is
remembered would seem to be legitimate. IOW, I may not recall that
wands of death are zinc, but I may recall that I only had one of them,
and it was in my bag, etc. Re-#naming items on the basis of such
recalled information is okay. It also runs the realistic risk of
misidentification.

>> Also, would it be cheating if I turned off the "assisting features" of
>> the interface and let it use the descriptive names (ELBIB YLOH, pink
>> potion, ...) for everything (with the `\' command left intact)? Then,
>> I would, just as the real character, learn to distinguish objects by
>> their use and would have little problems remembering what my stuff is
>> (the game will be a bit[?] more tedious however).
>
>It certainly removes the best-effort mechanism for amnesia, effectively
>making amnesia a non-issue, but I think the downside of having to
>remember everything without interface help is more than a balance.  

No. It's a horrible idea. Not only is it analogous to thinking
consciously "painted wooden stick" every time I refer to a pencil, but
a RL player has a life outside Nethack, which may tend to cause him to
forget what curved wands are within a particular game. Plus, it's
rather unlikely that a RL player is going to have his brain eaten by a
multi-tentacled monster.

Back to individual #naming. Since the game does view it as a type of
engraving, I propose that a character should actually have to use an
item to do it, which may be dulled in the process, and it should
consume in-game time. Of course, this can be circumvented by any
player who simply wants to jot the identities in his notes. But at
least it imposes some potential cost/risk, however slight, to
individual #naming, commensurate with the actual imagined practice of
spending time in the dungeon engraving information on every silly item
that you own.
